this arid landscape in northern kenya is  part of the greater horn of africa  that's experiencing its worst drought in  decades  as a result more than 1.7 million  children are suffering from the most  lethal form of malnutrition  we're hungry because we have nothing to  eat we only eat these wild berries we  have no livestock we have nothing we  depend on the trees where we collect  wild berries that's our food  the situation is being made worse by  conflict thousands of kilometers away  this life-saving supplement known as a  power pace can mean the difference  between life or death  in a place where aid workers say young  children are dying in near famine-like  conditions  invented a quarter of a century ago a  single sachet delivers 500 calories of  essential nutrients allowing  malnourished children to quickly regain  energy and weight  but the conflict in ukraine has affected  the cost and availability of the  life-saving peanut paste as well as the  means to deliver it to those who  desperately need it children die as a  result of malnutrition the effects of  drought  three years down the line no rain  so this one is life-saving  without this  mochi they will die  the timing could not be worse unicef  which buys nearly 80 of the world's  supply of these supplements expects the  cost to rise to 12 million dollars more  than when the war began  and with donations to deal with the food  shortage crisis here falling far behind  many say more needs to be done to help  the millions of people in the horn of  africa  jillian wolf al jazeera
 

